{
  "dom_id": "step-q274657",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[-1,0).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =0+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=-\\frac{1}{6}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
